title: $:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/sticky
tags: [[$:/tags/Stylesheet]]
code-body: yes

<!--
Sticky tiddler titles.

This lives in its own stylesheet tiddler rather than in base.tid because it
needs widgets: a reveal to honour the option, and a list to generate the
z-index ladder. base.tid restricts wikitext parsing with `\rules only` and so
cannot contain either.

The ladder is the non-obvious part. Once titles are sticky, a title scrolling
inside its own frame will pass UNDER the frame that follows it in the DOM,
because later siblings paint on top by default. Counting z-index downwards per
position reverses that, so each title stays above the tiddlers below it. 100
steps is the practical ceiling that Vanilla settled on.

The offset is --iw-menubar-h, NOT --iw-story-top.

Those two differ in kind. --iw-story-top is a layout offset: it pushes the
story river down inside the document, and scrolling consumes it — by the time
a title is sticking, the storytop gap has already scrolled off the screen.
Adding it here would hold the title that much too low forever, leaving a band
of dead space above every stuck title.

--iw-menubar-h is the part that is pinned to the VIEWPORT. That is the only
thing a sticky element has to clear, because it is the only thing still there
after scrolling. With no menubar the value is 0 and titles stick to the top of
the page, whatever storytop happens to be.

The negative inline margins let the title's background span the sheet's full
padding, so body text scrolls under an opaque strip instead of appearing in
the gutter beside it.
-->

<%if [{$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/options/stickytitles}match[yes]] %>
``
.tc-tiddler-title {
	position: sticky;
	inset-block-start: var(--iw-menubar-h, 0px);
	z-index: 500;
	background-color: ``<<colour tiddler-background>>``;

	/*
	Widen the strip from the sheet's content box out to its padding box, so
	body text scrolls under an opaque band rather than appearing beside it.

	These must mirror .tc-tiddler-frame's padding EXACTLY, so they read the
	same --iw-plate-pad-start token the frame does rather than restating the
	calculation. The frame's extra inline-start padding is unconditional — it
	reserves the margin whether or not the rule is drawn in it — so this
	compensation is unconditional too. Scoping it to the corrosion option left the
	strip 34px short on the inline start whenever the rule was switched off.

	Negative margins only widen a box whose inline-size is auto. Anything here
	that sets an explicit width silently turns this back into a shift.
	*/
	margin-inline: calc(-1 * var(--iw-plate-pad-start)) calc(-1 * var(--iw-space-lg));
	padding-inline: var(--iw-plate-pad-start) var(--iw-space-lg);
	padding-block: 2px;

	/*
	A stuck title is a label plate covering the face, not a gap in it.

	It has to stay OPAQUE — that is the whole mechanism, the thing body text
	scrolls under — so it can never show the patina behind it. Left flat, it
	reads as a hole punched in the corrosion the moment it sticks. These three
	lines make it read as a separate part instead:

	  dust        the fine grit tile ONLY, no craquelure and no stains. The
	              strip is pinned to the viewport while the plate scrolls
	              underneath, so anything drawn on it slides against the static
	              texture below — and fine, non-directional noise is the one
	              texture where that is imperceptible. Craquelure veins sliding
	              across craquelure veins are not.
	  light catch the same `inset 0 1px 0` every other cut edge in this theme
	              gets, so the strip sits ON the plate rather than in it.
	  hairline    a machined bottom edge, which is what tells you where the
	              part ends when the text below it is scrolling past.
	*/
	background-image: var(--iw-dust);
	background-size: 260px 260px;
	border-block-end: 1px solid var(--iw-plate-edge);
	box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 var(--iw-lit);
}

/*
A narrow river collapses the sheet to an even --iw-space-md padding and drops
the margin rule (see the `@container river` block in base.tid). The strip has
to follow that, or it overhangs the sheet by the gutter it no longer has.
*/
@container river (max-width: 34rem) {

	.tc-tiddler-title {
		margin-inline: calc(-1 * var(--iw-space-md));
		padding-inline: var(--iw-space-md);
	}
}

/*
Why the strip carries dust and nothing else is explained on the rule above.

The short version of what used to be here: while the plate wore its corrosion
in the leading margin, a stuck title wiped that band clean wherever it came to
rest, so the strip had to repaint the wash itself and line it up with the
frame's. The corrosion moved to the whole face, so there is no band to
continue — only a surface to avoid punching a hole in.
*/

``
<$list filter="[range[100]]">
`.tc-story-river .tc-tiddler-frame:nth-child(100n+`<$text text=<<currentTiddler>>/>`) {
	z-index: `<$text text={{{ [[200]subtract<currentTiddler>] }}}/>`;
}
`
</$list>
<% endif %>
